    modeller cut the shape out of the back panel and filled it with its own
    mesh carrying its own material, and the two share every boundary vertex
    to six decimal places. Deleting the insert would therefore have left a
    hole in the exact shape of the mark — the mark again, in negative — so
    its triangles were given the surrounding panel's material instead. What
    ships is one continuous, unmarked surface;
  - every material was renamed after the part it covers ("chassis", "trim",
    "camera-plate", "display-panel" and so on). The originals were named for
    a model designation, and a material name is the part of an asset most
    likely to be quoted in application code;
  - the studio ground plane was discarded;
  - the roughly 5 degree lean the scene was posed with was undone, and the
    model re-centred on its own bounding box and scaled to 0.72 units tall,
    then turned to face +Z;
  - the textures were rebuilt at 64 to 96 pixels and one normal map dropped.
    The original carried about 4 MB of 1024-square maps for a speaker grille
    weave, a mesh noise and a photograph of a flash lens, none of which is
    more than a few hundredths of a unit across at runtime. They were checked
    for marks first and carry none;
  - welded, decimated and quantised (KHR_mesh_quantization) for size;
  - the display rectangle was measured and recorded in asset.extras.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Replacing an asset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Every widget takes the asset it uses as a prop — <LaptopReveal> takes
`modelUrl` — so nothing here is baked in. If you point a widget at your own
file and stop shipping ours, that asset's obligations stop applying to your
build, and you can delete its entry below the line in your fork.

Do not delete an entry while still shipping the file it describes. For CC-BY
in particular, dropping the credit is the one thing the licence does not
allow, and a bundler will not warn you.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Adding an asset
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Before committing a new model, texture, font or sound:

  1. Check the licence actually permits redistribution inside an npm package,
     and commercial use. "Free to download" is not a licence. CC-BY, CC0 and
     the permissive code licences are fine; CC-BY-NC and most marketplace
     "standard" licences are not.

  2. Check it for third-party marks, and check all three places they hide. A
     model of a real product usually carries the manufacturer's logo in a
     texture; sometimes as its own mesh; and sometimes as a shape cut out of
     a panel and filled with an insert, where deleting the insert leaves the
     mark behind as a hole. A trademark is not covered by the asset's own
     licence however permissive that licence is. Remove the mark — filling
     or absorbing it, not merely deleting it — or do not ship the asset.

     Screen content counts too. A rendered home screen is a manufacturer's
     icons, UI and wordmark, and a photograph in a mockup is someone else's
     photograph.

  3. Add an entry above in the same shape: file path, which widget uses it,
     licence with URL, the credit verbatim as the author worded it, and every
     change made to the original. Record measurements the runtime depends on
     in asset.extras, as laptop.glb does.

  4. If the licence is share-alike (CC-BY-SA, GPL-family), stop and think
     first — it can reach further than the asset and is usually the wrong
     choice for a package other people embed.


